{"tool_name":"mmx-i","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"mmx-i","home":"https://bitbucket.org/antoinebergamaschi/mmx-i/overview","license":"MIT","summary":"MMX-I is a data-processing software for multimodal X-ray imaging and tomography.","addition_date":"2018-09-19T15:00:45Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:59:01Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:mmx-i","credit":[{"email":"antoine.bergamaschi@synchrotron-soleil.fr","name":"Antoine Bergamaschi","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://bitbucket.org/antoinebergamaschi/mmx-i/wiki/Home"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Image analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3443"}]}],"name":"MMX-I","operating_system":["Windows"],"owner":"daniel_stefan","publication":[{"doi":"10.1107/S1600577516003052","metadata":{"abstract":"A new multi-platform freeware has been developed for the processing and reconstruction of scanning multi-technique X-ray imaging and tomography datasets. The software platform aims to treat different scanning imaging techniques: X-ray fluorescence, phase, absorption and dark field and any of their combinations, thus providing an easy-to-use data processing tool for the X-ray imaging user community. A dedicated data input stream copes with the input and management of large datasets (several hundred GB) collected during a typical multi-technique fast scan at the Nanoscopium beamline and even on a standard PC. To the authors' knowledge, this is the first software tool that aims at treating all of the modalities of scanning multi-technique imaging and tomography experiments.","authors":[{"name":"Bergamaschi A."},{"name":"Marco S."},{"name":"Medjoubi K."},{"name":"Messaoudi C."},{"name":"Somogyi A."}],"citationCount":9,"date":"2016-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Journal of Synchrotron Radiation","title":"MMX-I: Data-processi